Panic and assert semantics
==========================

A small corpus that exercises the runtime semantics of `panic`, `assert`,
and clean-return paths. Useful as a backend smoke test — runs on all four
backends (`interpreter`, `llvm-host`, `svm-host`, `trisc`).

    module sysl.tests.panic_test


### clean return is a pass

`unit`-returning functions that complete normally must be reported as
passing tests across every backend.

    #test
    test_returns_cleanly() -> unit
        return


### panic is detected

A direct `panic("...")` call halts the program with an error condition
and must be classified as a panic across every backend.

    #test(should_panic: "boom")
    test_panic_is_caught() -> unit
        panic("boom")


### assert(false) is a panic

`assert(cond, "msg")` panics with the given message when `cond` is false.

    #test(should_panic: "fails")
    test_failing_assert() -> unit
        assert(false, "fails")


### assert(true) is a no-op

When `cond` is true the message is unused and execution continues.

    #test
    test_passing_assert() -> unit
        assert(true, "never panics")
